      "content": "In many established Hispanic syndicates, the relationship with law enforcement is often characterized by nuanced forms of transactional diplomacy and community infiltration, rather than outright confrontation. Recruitment might leverage existing social networks—family ties, church connections, or neighborhood debts—making it less about force and more about obligation and perceived camaraderie. Internal discipline often emphasizes strict adherence to a moral code and family hierarchy, where betrayal is seen as a profound familial disgrace rather than just a business lapse.",
      "source": "target",
